 </section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-pilot"><span class="anchor-alias" id="odor-screen-20261021"></span><p class="final-assay-kicker">05 · Odor testing</p><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Odor testing</h2><p>We followed fluorescence over time in ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and the control condition specified for each test. <strong class="media-reference">Figures 11–14</strong> show Test 1 and Test 2 time courses, condition comparisons, control-referenced maps and the Test 2 VUAA1-referenced map.</p><p>Test 2 contains 72 wells and 1,440 readings across ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and the blank control. Each construct × stimulus has one well.</p><p>VUAA1 is an established Orco agonist used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-jones" id="cite-p2-jones">[2]</a> <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-p2-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="11" id="fig-p2-traces"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b221621dfc17-integrated-vuaa1-traces-mobile.svg?v=b221621dfc17d6ce"/><img alt="VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 11. </strong>Normalized VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2. Test 1 uses each well’s paired baseline; Test 2 uses the first post-addition reading. Each trace represents one well, and panels have independent vertical scales.</figcaption></figure><p>In Test 2, ten of twelve VUAA1 time courses end above their first reading, while eleven conditions have a positive corrected change. Or7a + ORCO and the blank control end lower; a positive corrected score does not necessarily mean that the measured trace rises.</p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-controls"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">VUAA1 signals and control behavior</h3><p><strong class="media-reference">Figure 12</strong> compares all twelve cell conditions. In Test 2, corrected changes are +36.67 pp for Or98a + ORCO, +18.69 pp for ORCO only and −0.11 pp for the blank control.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="12" id="fig-p2-condition-comparison"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ef29c85a4d0d-integrated-condition-comparison-mobile.svg?v=ef29c85a4d0d0734"/><img alt="All twelve cell conditions in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 12. </strong>All twelve cell conditions. Test 1 shows peak test excursions (%), while Test 2 shows corrected changes (pp); these test-specific summaries are displayed separately.</figcaption></figure><p>Test 1 includes a large positive buffer-control signal, whereas the twelve Test 2 DMSO-control scores range from −1.79 to −0.31 pp. The corrected values are descriptive results from individual wells, rather than activation thresholds.</p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-map"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to assay controls</h3><p><strong class="media-reference">Figure 13</strong> adds the six-stimulus Test 2 screen to the original response maps. Test 2 corrected VUAA1 values range from −0.11 to +39.23 pp. Valeric acid spans −0.77 to +8.17 pp; the other three odor rows range from −2.06 to +1.36 pp. We show vehicle subtraction and the blank adjustment separately so that control-dependent changes remain visible.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="13" id="fig-p2-map"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"><img alt="Corrected response maps from both odor assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"/></a><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"><img alt="Vehicle and blank control contrasts from both assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 13. </strong>Response maps with Test 1 on the left and Test 2 on the right, receptors in rows and stimuli in columns, on a shared −60 to +60 pp scale. The first ten receptor labels denote OR + ORCO; hatched Test 1 isopentyl-acetate cells remain excluded, and separate-well control subtraction does not establish receptor specificity.</figcaption></figure><div id="vuaa1-referenced-response"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to each construct’s VUAA1 reference</h3><p><strong class="media-reference">Figure 14</strong> compares each Test 2 odor response with the VUAA1 reference for the same construct, after subtracting its DMSO control. Construct-specific VUAA1 referencing has been used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-vuaa1-reference-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated final-assay-figure--reference" data-static-figure-number="14" id="fig-vuaa1-reference"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"><img alt="Test 2 odor responses normalized to the DMSO-subtracted VUAA1 response of each construct" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 14. </strong>Test 2 VUAA1-referenced responses, with DMSO set to 0 and VUAA1 to 1 for each construct and one well per condition; Or7a–Or98a coexpress ORCO. Orange cells retain negative ratios; †Or7a has a small VUAA1 reference (2.19 pp), making its ratios particularly sensitive to that reference.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
+<section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="f1-dynamic-door">
+<h2 class="reading-heading--chapter"><dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> dynamics and the DoOR reference</h2>
+<p>A separate analysis of the retained <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> readings compares four odorant profiles across ten OR + ORCO conditions with the DoOR receptor database. The two views below place measured, vehicle-referenced fluorescence changes beside the receptor patterns used to inform our decoding work. The analysis uses the experimenter-confirmed channel labels and source-cell mapping in the <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> workbook; it reuses the Test 2 <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> records.</p>
+<p><strong class="media-reference">Figure 15</strong> shows distinct profiles across the four odorants. Valeric acid has the largest positive contrast, +6.97 percentage points (pp) for the supplied Or22a channel, followed by +3.02 pp for Or67a and +2.98 pp for Or35a. Isopentyl acetate is highest for Or42a (+1.35 pp), while pentyl acetate is highest for Or67a (+1.74 pp). Values retain their signs and are shown on a common fluorescence scale.</p>
+<figure class="wiki-figure" data-static-figure-number="15" id="fig-f1-door-heatmaps"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/dynamic-and-door-heatmaps.svg"><img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/dynamic-and-door-heatmaps.svg" alt="Four odorant profiles across ten receptors: F1 dynamic changes minus matched DMSO, alongside DoOR consensus minus receptor spontaneous firing rate" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 15. </strong><dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn>-only odorant-minus-DMSO contrasts (top, pp) and DoOR consensus minus receptor-specific spontaneous firing rate, SFR (bottom, DoOR units). The scales differ between panels. Each experimental condition has one well; all ten tuning OR conditions coexpress ORCO. The grey cell is the missing Or42a–valeric acid DoOR value. The fluorescence contrast compares early and late three-reading means; <a href="experiments.html#exp-f1-dynamic">Experiments gives the calculation</a>. DoOR-derived material: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/srep21841">Münch and Galizia, 2016</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>.</figcaption></figure>
+<p>The across-receptor comparisons show weak positive association with DoOR for 2-heptanone (Pearson r = +0.317; Spearman ρ = +0.406), isopentyl acetate (r = +0.325; ρ = +0.442) and valeric acid (r = +0.211; ρ = +0.283). Pentyl acetate is close to zero (r = −0.002; ρ = +0.006). Thus, the experimental profiles share part of the database ordering for three odorants, with agreement depending on the odorant.</p>
+<figure class="wiki-figure" data-static-figure-number="16" id="fig-f1-door-scatter"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/dynamic-door-scatter.svg"><img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/dynamic-door-scatter.svg" alt="Per-odorant scatter plots comparing F1 dynamic contrasts with DoOR minus spontaneous firing rate, with Pearson and Spearman correlations" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 16. </strong>Each point is one receptor–odorant pair. Correlations are calculated separately within each odorant: n = 10 receptors, except valeric acid (n = 9 because Or42a is missing from DoOR). ORCO-only and blank controls are retained in the analysis package and excluded from these tuning-OR correlations. Values use the supplied three-point-window calculation. DoOR-derived material: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/srep21841">Münch and Galizia, 2016</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>.</figcaption></figure>
+</section>
 <section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="limitations"><span class="anchor-alias" id="discussion"></span><span class="anchor-alias" id="negative-inconclusive"></span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Discussion</h2><p>The Test 2 VUAA1 time courses show within-acquisition rises in most receptor conditions. After subtracting each well’s F0 change, eleven conditions are positive and the blank value is close to zero. Test 1 and Test 2 use different control designs and calculations, so they provide separate observations rather than interchangeable estimates.</p><p>In Test 1, the ORCO-only VUAA1 well showed an 11.86% fluorescence excursion, compared with 0.98% in the untransfected well. The 35.25% excursion in a buffer-only well shows why a rise alone cannot establish ligand-specific activation.</p><p>Colony growth and undigested-plasmid migration document preparation, but neither establishes insert sequence or construct identity. Separate RED and GREEN images likewise do not establish receptor localization or channel function.</p><p>GCaMP6f is a calcium-sensitive indicator. Its resting fluorescence is not a constitutive, calcium-independent measure of transfection. Low green signal may reflect indicator state, expression, acquisition sensitivity or background, and a single endpoint cannot distinguish these explanations. Functional interpretation requires time-resolved, controlled stimulation. <a aria-label="Reference 1" class="cite" href="#micro-ref-1" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-1-1">[1]</a></p><p>The fixed-cell screen at 44–48 h and Test 1 at approximately 72 h are separate pre-selection records. Their position in the workflow does not establish that they came from the same cell batch.</p>
-<p>The microscopy fractions all come from one experiment. Uneven field coverage, within-field clustering and density exclusions prevent ranking receptor conditions as independently replicated estimates of transfection or expression. Blank and Orco-only controls provide within-study context, but do not supply biological replication.</p><p>Test 1 used a laboratory plate reader. These results characterize the biological assay; they do not establish the performance of the assembled AeroSense reader.</p><p>Control comparisons use separate single wells and describe fluorescence patterns rather than receptor-specific activation. Test 2 includes a DMSO reference; Test 1 lacks a matched vehicle control. Unrecorded gain, cell-state variation and dilution-only concentration records limit interpretation. Vehicle-matched tests at documented doses in independent cultures are needed to separate these effects.</p></section>
-<section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="conclusion"><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Conclusion</h2><p>The expression screen detected reporter-associated fluorescence above the blank-derived threshold in resolvable DAPI objects. RED-positive fractions across the OR + ORCO conditions ranged from 0.7% to 16.5%; dense overlap prevents interpreting these fractions as whole-culture transfection efficiency.</p><p>The odor assays provide time-resolved evidence of VUAA1-associated fluorescence. In Test 2, F0-subtracted VUAA1 changes are +36.67 pp for Or98a + ORCO, +17.52 pp for Or85b + ORCO, +29.46 pp for Or35a + ORCO, and +18.69 pp for ORCO only, compared with −0.11 pp in the blank control. The control maps and retained raw records make these observations inspectable.</p></section>
+<p>The microscopy fractions all come from one experiment. Uneven field coverage, within-field clustering and density exclusions prevent ranking receptor conditions as independently replicated estimates of transfection or expression. Blank and Orco-only controls provide within-study context, but do not supply biological replication.</p><p>Test 1 used a laboratory plate reader. These results characterize the biological assay; they do not establish the performance of the assembled AeroSense reader.</p><p>Control comparisons use separate single wells and describe fluorescence patterns rather than receptor-specific activation. Test 2 includes a DMSO reference; Test 1 lacks a matched vehicle control. Unrecorded gain, cell-state variation and dilution-only concentration records limit interpretation. Vehicle-matched tests at documented doses in independent cultures are needed to separate these effects.</p><p id="f1-dynamic-discussion">The <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn>-only comparison provides an exploratory connection between the cell measurements and the DoOR reference used in Model. Its metric is C = D<sub>odor</sub> − D<sub>DMSO</sub>, based on early and late <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> means; it is distinct from the F0-subtracted S values and VUAA1-referenced N ratios above. Positive C can represent a smaller decline than DMSO, rather than a rising trace. The experimenter confirmed matched DMSO fraction and addition volume, while gain consistency and addition-to-first-read delay remain unconfirmed. The experimenter-confirmed <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn> channel map supersedes the earlier Test 2 receptor labels; the underlying fluorescence readings are unchanged.</p><p>The first three odorants show weak positive cross-platform agreement, while pentyl acetate does not. Leaving one receptor out gives Pearson ranges of +0.080 to +0.502, +0.198 to +0.488, +0.169 to +0.550 and −0.284 to +0.182, respectively. These ranges describe sensitivity to receptor selection, not confidence intervals. One well per condition, shared DMSO references and different measurement platforms limit transfer claims; timepoints do not increase biological n. This reanalysis does not provide an independent experimental validation, receptor-specific activation test or demonstrated fluorescence-to-odor classifier.</p></section>
+<section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="conclusion"><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Conclusion</h2><p>The expression screen detected reporter-associated fluorescence above the blank-derived threshold in resolvable DAPI objects. RED-positive fractions across the OR + ORCO conditions ranged from 0.7% to 16.5%; dense overlap prevents interpreting these fractions as whole-culture transfection efficiency.</p><p>The odor assays provide time-resolved evidence of VUAA1-associated fluorescence. In Test 2, F0-subtracted VUAA1 changes are +36.67 pp for Or98a + ORCO, +17.52 pp for Or85b + ORCO, +29.46 pp for Or35a + ORCO, and +18.69 pp for ORCO only, compared with −0.11 pp in the blank control. The control maps and retained raw records make these observations inspectable.</p><p>The <dfn title="Post-addition fluorescence acquisition">F1</dfn>-only reanalysis adds odorant-dependent comparisons with DoOR: weak positive across-receptor associations for three odorants and near-zero association for pentyl acetate. The shared reference makes the measured profiles and their correspondence to the decoding database directly inspectable.</p></section>
 <section class="section-block data-availability" data-reveal="" id="data-availability"><span class="anchor-alias" id="methods"></span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Data availability</h2><p class="publication-status">Code, analysis scripts and reusable documentation are published in the <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense" rel="noopener noreferrer">official iGEM GitLab repository</a>. Archive availability: <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/docs/aerosense/archive-status.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Research archive availability</a>.</p><details class="supplementary-data__group" id="results-supplement-sources"><summary>Source data and documents</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body"><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-odor21-source-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Test 2 odor-assay archive</h3><p>The complete Test 2 raw-data package is prepared locally for Zenodo; no public deposit has been verified. The package contains twelve unchanged XLSX exports, checksums, 1,440 observations with source-cell references, 72 corrected summaries, matched-window sensitivity values and reproducible analysis scripts. The earlier mean-ratio calculation is retained only as a secondary audit metric. All corrected values are available in the supplementary tables.</p><p><a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/tree/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/data/odor-2026-10-21/analysis">Test 2 correction and VUAA1-reference source</a></p></div><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-complete-curves-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Complete odor-assay curves</h3><p>The complete-curve analysis preserves the original acquisition boundaries and checks every plotted reading against the canonical exports. The scalable figure, chart-data tables and endpoint audit accompany the single reproducible builder.</p><p><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/102cb9b79ee9-odor-odor-complete-timecourses.svg">Full-size vector figure · SVG</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/a81f28b9d83f-odor-raw-curves.csv">All 5,280 raw curve readings · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/6114088f51d3-odor-test2-vuaa1-referenced-curves.csv">Test 2 referenced time courses · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/f97531b3bf7e-odor-test2-endpoint-audit.csv"><strong class="media-reference">Figure 14</strong> endpoint checks · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/c6c99f96269b-odor-test1-peak-metric-audit.csv">Test 1 peak-metric checks · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/f536163e8819-odor-source-manifest.json">Source checksums · JSON</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/data/odor-complete-curves-2026-10-21/analysis/build_complete_curves.py">Complete-curve analysis source</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/data/odor-complete-curves-2026-10-21/README.md">Complete-curve reproduction guide</a></p></div><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-p2-source-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Test 1 kinetic assay</h3><p><span class="publication-sources">Test 1 source and archive — <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/tree/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/assets/results/final-test" rel="noopener noreferrer">Test 1 analysis source — GitLab</a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/docs/aerosense/archive-status.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Full raw-data archive availability</a></span> — the complete archive preserves 32 exact instrument exports, filename map, source analysis, tidy data, independent calculations, English figures and executed methods. The numerical analysis uses only Python’s standard library. Original inputs are preserved, including the source report whose claims are audited.</p><p><a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/docs/aerosense/archive-status.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">3,840-row tidy time series — archive availability</a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/257ea425bbdd-plate-p2-well-summary.csv" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">96-well summaries</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/raw/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/assets/results/final-test/analyze_plate_p2.py" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Analysis script</span> — <span>GitLab</span></a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/e20f5ca1d570-experiment-metadata.json" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Metadata and provenance</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/b826bffef90c-plate-p2-methods.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">Test 1 executed methods · PDF</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/raw/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/assets/results/final-test/README.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Reproduction guide</span> — <span>GitLab</span></a></p><p><a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/e6661eba50f5-plate-p2-source-number-audit.csv" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Numerical audit</a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/59c0111dd66c-plate-p2-run-qc.csv" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Transparent QC table</a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/19aa89f1ecb9-plate-p2-source-qc-audit.csv" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">QC label audit</a></p><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Endpoint microscopy</h3></div>
 <p>The dataset contains 100 individual-channel images across 25 fields, 12 pre-existing merges and the experimental workbook. Individual channels are 2592 × 1944 pixels; fluorescence images are 8-bit pseudocolored exports. Measurements use the individual channels and the condition and field identifiers in the supplied source folders. The WL-MIC-01 reanalysis retains the original acquisition and single-experiment scope. Acquisition metadata, processing parameters and source-file checksums accompany the analysis.</p>
