 </div></details><details class="supplementary-data__group" id="results-supplement-measurement"><summary>Measurement audit</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body"><section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="control-comparison"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">What the controls show</h3><p>The control comparison asks whether the object-calling procedure separates reporter-associated signal from the blank within this image set. We recomputed the primary counts from the object-level CSV, retaining the positive-object numerator and eligible-object denominator before comparing each nonblank condition with the untransfected control. Eight of ten OR-labelled conditions have nominal RED Holm-adjusted values below 0.05 under an independent-object model. Or22a (0.080) and Or98a (1.000) do not cross that diagnostic cutoff. These values describe the selected image objects under the stated statistical model.</p>
 <p><strong>Interpretation boundary.</strong> These are post-hoc, exploratory object-level calculations. Objects share fields and a single experiment, so the independence assumption is not satisfied; the blank also fitted the positivity threshold. Neither Fisher testing nor Holm correction repairs pseudoreplication or that calibration reuse. We report the values to make the comparison inspectable, without biological-significance stars or population claims. The full <a href="measurement.html#control-statistics">statistical specification</a> is on Measurement.</p><div aria-label="Data table; scroll horizontally to inspect all columns." class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="goal-table" id="table-red-controls"><caption><strong class="media-caption__label">Table 4. </strong>RED comparison with blank (1/170); one-sided Fisher model, Holm over 11 comparisons within this channel.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">RED+ / eligible</th><th scope="col">RED fraction</th><th scope="col">Difference from blank (percentage points)</th><th scope="col">Nominal Fisher p</th><th scope="col">Nominal Holm p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Or19a+ORCO</th><td>25/242</td><td>10.33%</td><td>+9.74</td><td>1.2e-05</td><td>8.8e-05</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or22a+ORCO</th><td>8/189</td><td>4.23%</td><td>+3.64</td><td>0.027</td><td>0.080</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or35a+ORCO</th><td>28/200</td><td>14.00%</td><td>+13.41</td><td>1.9e-07</td><td>1.7e-06</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or42a+ORCO</th><td>18/263</td><td>6.84%</td><td>+6.26</td><td>0.00084</td><td>0.004</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or59b+ORCO</th><td>38/230</td><td>16.52%</td><td>+15.93</td><td>3.3e-09</td><td>3.7e-08</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or67a+ORCO</th><td>17/161</td><td>10.56%</td><td>+9.97</td><td>3.1e-05</td><td>0.00019</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or67b+ORCO</th><td>14/193</td><td>7.25%</td><td>+6.67</td><td>0.0009</td><td>0.004</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or7a+ORCO</th><td>21/192</td><td>10.94%</td><td>+10.35</td><td>1.1e-05</td><td>8.8e-05</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or85b+ORCO</th><td>25/156</td><td>16.03%</td><td>+15.44</td><td>5.1e-08</td><td>5.1e-07</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or98a+ORCO</th><td>2/286</td><td>0.70%</td><td>+0.11</td><td>0.687</td><td>1.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ORCO only</th><td>0/342</td><td>0.00%</td><td>-0.59</td><td>1.000</td><td>1.000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In GREEN, only Orco-only (24/342; nominal Holm p = 0.00485) crosses the same descriptive cutoff. Or35a has 11/200 positive objects (5.5%; nominal Holm p = 0.063) across two technical fields and does not cross that cutoff. The discussion separates indicator abundance, calcium state and receptor function. <a href="#results-source-controls">Control comparison files</a>.</p>
-<p>The nominal 95% Wilson limits in the download quantify binomial counting uncertainty only under independent-object sampling. They exclude field clustering, threshold fitting, staining incompleteness and segmentation bias; they are not confidence intervals for a culture’s transfection efficiency.</p></section><section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="measurement-disagreement"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Bright area is not localized signal</h3><p>An image can look broadly fluorescent even when little signal is locally distinguishable around a resolved object. To examine that difference, <strong class="media-reference">Figure 15</strong> compares the median field-level fluorescent-area percentage with the pooled positive-object fraction. The area denominator is the analyzed image pixels outside the fixed annotation exclusion; the object denominator is the eligible DAPI objects across the acquired fields. This asks whether broad area and localized object contrast vary together, rather than assuming they measure the same property.</p>
+<p>The nominal 95% Wilson limits in the download quantify binomial counting uncertainty only under independent-object sampling. They exclude field clustering, threshold fitting, staining incompleteness and segmentation bias; they are not confidence intervals for a culture’s transfection efficiency.</p></section><section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="measurement-disagreement"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Bright area is not localized signal</h3><p>An image can look broadly fluorescent even when little signal is locally distinguishable around a resolved object. To examine that difference, <strong class="media-reference">Figure 17</strong> compares the median field-level fluorescent-area percentage with the pooled positive-object fraction. The area denominator is the analyzed image pixels outside the fixed annotation exclusion; the object denominator is the eligible DAPI objects across the acquired fields. This asks whether broad area and localized object contrast vary together, rather than assuming they measure the same property.</p>
 <p>Across 12 conditions, median fluorescent area and pooled object fractions have RED Spearman ρ = 0.23 (descriptive p = 0.47) and GREEN ρ = −0.02 (p = 0.95). Within the ten receptor labels, the corresponding ρ values are −0.32 and 0.006. Small samples, shared acquisition and different denominators prevent a precise agreement claim.</p>
