</td><td>+0.31</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ORCO only</th><td>+8.85</td><td>-0.20</td><td>+0.58</td><td>+0.17</td><td>+0.64</td><td>+0.37</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Blank</th><td>-7.83</td><td>+0.09</td><td>+0.05</td><td>+0.96</td><td>+0.81</td><td>+0.32</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div id="vuaa1-reference-method"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">VUAA1-reference normalization</h3><p>For Test 2, S is the F0-subtracted change defined above. For each construct c and stimulus s, N(s,c) = [S(s,c) − S(DMSO,c)] / [S(VUAA1,c) − S(DMSO,c)]. This sets the measured DMSO and VUAA1 reference wells to 0 and 1; it does not impose a biological response bound. Negative ratios and ratios above 1 remain valid numerical outcomes and are not clipped.</p><p>All eleven Test 2 receptor-containing denominators are positive (2.19–39.54 pp). Seven of the 44 odor ratios are negative, and none exceeds 1. Or7a’s denominator is only 2.19 pp, compared with at least 9.43 pp for the other constructs; its valeric-acid ratio of 0.972 comes from a 2.13 pp vehicle-subtracted response. The blank control is omitted from the construct-referenced ratio.</p><p>Test 1 is not shown in this normalized map: eight of its eleven buffer-referenced VUAA1 denominators are nonpositive, and its buffer row is not a matched DMSO vehicle. Direct and control-referenced ratios for both tests are retained in the research audit. These single-well ratios cannot remove well-to-well transfection differences or supply uncertainty estimates.</p><div aria-label="Test 2 VUAA1-referenced ratios" class="table-scroll" id="vuaa1-reference-table" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="goal-table"><caption>Test 2 VUAA1-referenced ratios</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Construct</th><th scope="col">VUAA1</th><th scope="col">Isopentyl acetate</th><th scope="col">2-Heptanone</th><th scope="col">Valeric acid</th><th scope="col">Pentyl acetate</th><th scope="col">DMSO control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Or7a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.314</td><td>0.192</td><td>0.972</td><td>0.342</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or19a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.051</td><td>0.067</td><td>0.157</td><td>0.028</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or42a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.140</td><td>0.049</td><td>0.151</td><td>0.092</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or98a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.020</td><td>0.038</td><td>0.084</td><td>0.012</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or22a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>-0.010</td><td>-0.016</td><td>0.214</td><td>-0.001</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or67a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.034</td><td>0.032</td><td>0.201</td><td>0.141</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or67b + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>-0.030</td><td>0.040</td><td>0.036</td><td>0.042</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or59b + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>-0.031</td><td>0.010</td><td>0.003</td><td>-0.015</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or85b + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.019</td><td>0.056</td><td>0.093</td><td>0.027</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Or35a + ORCO</th><td>1.000</td><td>0.012</td><td>0.020</td><td>0.103</td><td>0.033</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ORCO only</th><td>1.000</td><td>-0.058</td><td>0.017</td><td>0.018</td><td>0.013</td><td>0.000</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div id="odor-processing-details"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Processing definitions</h3><p>For Test 2, we calculate the endpoint change within each acquisition and subtract the F0 change from the F1 change. S is expressed in percentage points (pp). For Or98a + ORCO with VUAA1, +34.09% − (−2.57%) gives +36.67 pp using unrounded values. ORCO only gives +18.69 pp, while the blank control gives −0.11 pp.</p><p class="assay-formula"><code>ΔF1 = 100 × (F1last / F1first − 1)</code><br/><code>ΔF0 = 100 × (F0last / F0first − 1)</code><br/><code>S = ΔF1 − ΔF0</code></p><p>The Test 2 subtraction compares a 168 s F1 window with a 48 s F0 window. It is a descriptive disturbance correction, not a fitted bleaching correction. A matched 0–48 s calculation is included in the numerical audit: the eleven receptor-containing VUAA1 conditions remain positive, while the blank value is −7.83 pp. For VUAA1, the shorter window preserves the direction but changes the magnitude.</p><p>Control behavior remains important. In Test 1, the Or35a + ORCO buffer-only well had a 35.25% excursion. In Test 2, all twelve DMSO-control S values lie between −1.79 and −0.31 pp. Or7a + ORCO has a small positive S of +0.40 pp even though its F1 signal falls by 1.47%, because F0 falls further. These examples show why both subtraction components accompany the corrected values.</p><details class="p2-comparison-method"><summary>Calculation and interpretation</summary><p><code>B(s,c) = S(s,c) − S(DMSO,c)</code><br/><code>C(s,c) = B(s,c) − B(s,blank)</code></p><p>For Test 1, the same between-well operations use its buffer-corrected excursion and buffer-only row. The four-acquisition Test 1 protocol and the two-acquisition Test 2 protocol have different within-well definitions; their magnitudes are not interchangeable. The original earlier numerical tables are retained in the audit.</p></details><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a2d81909a16c-supplementary-correction-components.svg?v=a2d81909a16c5f32">View the full processing components</a></div></div></details></section>
